/*  doWhileNameT -- THE NEW PARSE ROAD CRASHES WHEN A DO's WHILE EXPRESSION IS NOT A
    BARE LITERAL. Born RED on purpose, 2026-09-20, Clay's SEQ 171 step 1, oracle twin
    added under SEQ 172 amendment 1.

    ⚠ THE ROW IS A PIN, NOT A DIAGNOSIS. What is asserted is that the run reaches its
    foot. It does not today: the target drives die at EXC_BAD_ACCESS in aCTionTokenXP,
    reading a null ANYorNum label. The rows go green when the label channel lands.

    ⚠⚠ THE DECLARED-COUNTER TWIN IS WHY `result` IS NOT THE VARIABLE. DW-4 uses a field
    declared in this file and it dies exactly as DW-5 does, so the crash is not about an
    unassigned name. DW-5 stays pinned anyway, because it is the drive string the report
    and the ladder were measured on and a pin that quietly changes its subject is not the
    same pin.

    ⚠ DW-1 IS THE ORACLE AND IT MUST RUN BEFORE ANY parser CALL. Once DO carries a
    generated parse there is no way back to the old road inside one process, so the
    banked answer has to be taken first. It prints one line: 1.

    ⚠ THE ORACLE'S VALUE IS ECHOED TO STDERR AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT STYLE. This
    file crashes, and a crash discards block-buffered stdout -- so the oracle's printed
    line is GONE from the capture even though it really was produced. stderr is
    unbuffered and survives, so the counter is read back through it. Assert the value
    there and never from the stdout of a run that dies.

    ⚠⚠ AND THE COUNTER IS READ BARE, WHICH INVERTS THE USUAL ADVICE AND IS MEASURED.
    A capture of it -- dwCap taken with the group-set operator -- prints its own tag
    both before and after the drive, so it carries no value and would pin nothing. The
    BARE read answers correctly: 0 before the drive and 1 after. That is also the proof
    that the drive string and this file share ONE node, which is the fact the row is
    really asserting.

    ⚠ AND WHAT THE ORACLE ACTUALLY SHOWS IS ONE PASS, NOT A LOOP. Driven on the old road
    the body runs exactly once and returns, for every condition tried -- under 2, under 4,
    and under a constant true. Whether that is do-while not iterating or a rule-drive not
    executing a loop is NOT MEASURED and nothing here claims either. It is banked as the
    oracle because it is what the old road does, which is all an oracle has to be.

    ⚠ THE CONTROL ROW IS NOT DECORATION AND IT IS WHY THIS FIXTURE IS ONE FILE. Every
    drive below DW-2 runs under THE SAME generated parse -- one generation, four drive
    strings -- so a red target cannot be blamed on generation. The control returns first
    and prints a marker, so a run that dies before reaching a target fails the control row
    too and the pin cannot be read as satisfied by a fixture that never got started.  */
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search GroupFields UnitTests Utilities list;
define dwN=0;
    ;

cerr "DW-1 oracle -- the declared-counter twin on the OLD road":;
DO("do print ++dwN; while dwN < 2;");
cerr "DW-1 ORACLE RETURNED dwN=" dwN:;

cerr "DW-2 parser generates for DO":;
parser(DO);

cerr "DW-3 control -- a literal while expression":;
DO("do print 1; while 0;");
cerr "DW-3 CONTROL RETURNED":;

cerr "DW-4 target -- the declared-counter twin on the NEW road":;
DO("do print ++dwN; while dwN < 2;");
cerr "DW-4 dwN AFTER =" dwN:;
cerr "DW-4 TWIN RETURNED":;

cerr "DW-5 target -- an unassigned name in the while expression":;
DO("do print ++result; while result < 2;");
cerr "DW-5 TARGET RETURNED":;

cerr "DOWHILENAME SENTINEL":;
stop();

This text is past stop(); the parse halts there and nothing below is read.

/*****************************************************************************
                    What DW-4 And DW-5 Are Pinning
*****************************************************************************/
    The crash, with frames, measured 2026-09-20 on a bare binary, canary 335:

        EXC_BAD_ACCESS (code=1, address=0x0)
        aCTionTokenXP(xpress=0x...) at GroupRules.mm:1265
            if ( isGROUP(ANYtoken->groupBody->flags.data) )

    Bear-trap 36 applies and the line that died is not the line that read null. The
    PRODUCER is GroupRules.mm:1249, ruleActions.rtn:987:

        GroupItem ANYtoken = xpress["ANYorNum"];

    xpress is NOT null -- it carries a real address. What is missing is its ANYorNum
    member, which is Tony's "TokenXP receives xpress with no list" said from the other
    end. The sub-term's label never attached, so the seat reads the slot as null and
    dereferences it two lines later.

    The generated body TokenXP now carries is

        return UnaryOPS() && ANYorNum() && InvokeArg();

    against a grammar line whose first and third terms are OPTIONAL. The emitted body
    carries no modifiers -- the known F-89 seam -- so two optional terms are conjuncts
    that must succeed.

/*****************************************************************************
                    The Ladder That Named The Variable
*****************************************************************************/
    One run each, same generated parse, the drive string the only thing changed, alarm
    at sixty seconds. Exit 139 is the crash; exit 0 reached the sentinel.

        A   do print 1; while 0;                 exit 0
        B   do print 1; while 1 less 0;          exit 0
        C   do print 1; while result less 2;     exit 139
        D   do print ++result; while 0;          exit 0
        E   do print ++result; while result less 2;   exit 139
        F   do print 1; while not result less 2; exit 139
        G   do print 1; while result;            exit 139
        H   do print 1; while not 1;             exit 139
        I   do print 1; while not result;        exit 139

    D is the row that matters most and it is easy to miss: the bump and the name are
    fine in the STATEMENT BODY. Only the while EXPRESSION kills it. So the variable is
    not the bump, not the comparison, and not the name as such -- A and B pass with a
    comparison of literals, and every row that routes the while expression through
    TokenXP dies.

    ⚠ H IS A COUNTEREXAMPLE AND IT IS RECORDED BECAUSE IT KILLS THE TIDIEST READING.
    The obvious story is that an ABSENT optional unary returns falsy, short-circuits
    the conjunction, and leaves ANYorNum unasked. H supplies a unary on a literal --
    present, not absent -- and still dies. So a short-circuit at an absent UnaryOPS
    cannot be the whole cause. What that positive cause IS has not been measured here,
    and nothing in this file claims one. Clay re-cut the question under SEQ 172: read
    DISPATCH rather than exit status, once xpress survives long enough to be dumped.
